      "message": "feat(turning): read the lathe axis by consensus when a large keyway fails the plain gate\n\nThe axis fit claimed an axis only when 90 % of the answering surface met\nthe plain least-squares line, and the #71 robust pass ran only after\nthat gate. A large keyway or flat failed it first: a 10 mm x 20 mm\nkeyway 6 mm deep on a D20 shaft drags the plain line 0.57 mm off,\ntwice the sagitta band, and a 50 mm keyway sets aside 17 % of the\nsurface. Both were refused as \"share no rotation axis\".\n\nWhen no candidate passes the plain gate, motion's new axis_consensus\npass fits each candidate direction again by consensus. It keeps the\nfacets whose normal lines meet the line within a MAD-scaled cut that is\nat least halved each round down to the sagitta band, and it holds the\ncandidate's direction so that a pencil of prism faces cannot swing it.\nIt then reads the gate about the consensus line at 75 % of the\nanswering area (CONSENSUS_SHARE), with the coarseness and pencil gates\nunchanged, and hands the line to the robust reweighting at that share.\nParts that pass the plain gate never reach the fallback, so their fits\nand the goldens are unchanged. An off-axis lump carrying 30 % of the\nsurface, a box, and #208's plate with a boss are still refused by name.\n\nThe spindle law's refusal text moves into `refusal()` so that the\nuntrusted (biased-fit) message is pinned by unit tests.\n\nfeat, not fix: the old refusal was safe and by design, and this turns\nparts that could not be turned before.\n\nFixes #113\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
